/-!
# THE SYNTACTIC BRIDGE: how a WIRE REMAP acts on the ENCODING

`ShiEmbed.embedInstr`/`embedLayer`/`embedCirc` relabel wires; `ShiBQP.encInstr`/`encLayer`/
`encCirc` write a circuit down as a bit string.  Every accepted theorem about the embedding
is SEMANTIC (`runLayered`, `acceptProb`, `depth`, `LayerOk`); none of them mentions
`ShiBQP.encInstr`, and the source comment on `embedLayer` explicitly says that the
order-preservation of the `List.map` is left unproved.  So a machine that must emit
`encCirc (ampFan2 ++ ampFan3 ++ embedCirc e₁ (F.circ n) ++ ...)` has, as of now, no equation
relating the encoding of an EMBEDDED instruction to the encoding of the original one.

This file supplies that equation, in the three shapes an emitter actually consumes.

## 1.  Per instruction: the TAG is untouched, the INDEX is remapped

Stated constructor by constructor, because an emitter dispatches on the tag:

    encInstr (embedInstr e he (.h i))          = encNat 0 ++ encNat (e i)
    encInstr (embedInstr e he (.s i))          = encNat 1 ++ encNat (e i)
    encInstr (embedInstr e he (.t i))          = encNat 2 ++ encNat (e i)
    encInstr (embedInstr e he (.x i))          = encNat 3 ++ encNat (e i)
    encInstr (embedInstr e he (.cnot i j hij)) = encNat 4 ++ encNat (e i) ++ encNat (e j)

BOTH `cnot` indices are remapped.  All five hold by `rfl`: `embedInstr` and `encInstr` match
on the same five constructors and neither looks at the `cnot` proof field (a `Prop`, hence
irrelevant), so the two matches compose to a single iota reduction.  The `cnot` branch still
has to be written out explicitly -- a catch-all `_` would not refine the constructor and the
rebuilt proof `fun h => hij (he h)` would not elaborate (RESUME gotcha 231).

## 2.  Per layer and per circuit: the LENGTH PREFIXES ARE COPIED VERBATIM

`encLayer l = encNat (l.map encInstr).length ++ (l.map encInstr).flatten`, so a naive emitter
would have to RECOMPUTE the length prefix of an embedded layer.  It does not have to:

    encLayer (embedLayer e he l) = encNat l.length ++ (l.map (encInstr ∘ embedInstr e he)).flatten
    encCirc  (embedCirc e he c)  = encNat c.length ++ (c.map (encLayer ∘ embedLayer e he)).flatten

with `l.length`, `c.length` the counts of the ORIGINAL layer/circuit.  Together with
`(embedLayer e he l).length = l.length`, `(embedCirc e he c).length = c.length`,
`depth (embedCirc e he c) = depth c` and the per-layer instruction counts
`(embedCirc e he c).map List.length = c.map List.length`, this is exactly the statement that
a HEADER EMITTER may copy a header it has already produced rather than recount anything: the
remap changes no length anywhere in the encoding tree.

## 3.  The length identity

The per-constructor lengths are not re-derived here; they are the third through seventh
conjuncts of `ShiTM.encoding_length_formulas_and_size_bound` (SIZE-1), applied at the
AMBIENT wire count `n` and at the REMAPPED index, which is legitimate exactly because of
part 1.  So `(encInstr (embedInstr e he (.h i))).length = e i + 2`, and the `cnot` case
`e i + e j + 7` moves with both indices.

## Non-vacuity

The concrete conjunct is unconditional: `e : Fin 2 → Fin 5`, `e i = i + 2`, injective, and
the five instructions `.h 0`, `.s 1`, `.t 0`, `.x 1`, `.cnot 0 1` are encoded at LITERAL bit
lists.  For `.cnot 0 1` the value is

    [true,true,true,true,false, true,true,false, true,true,true,false]
     \______ tag 4 ______/      \_ index 2 _/    \__ index 3 ___/

i.e. wires `0` and `1` are BOTH visibly shifted to `2` and `3`; the unshifted encoding would
have been `... [false] [true,false]`.  A tag-preservation claim without a shifted index on
display is worth very little, so the literals are part of the public statement.

## Nothing here is semantic

No claim is made about `runLayered`, `acceptProb` or `LayerOk` -- those are the accepted
embed theorems and are untouched.  This file is about bits.
-/

/-! ### Two list lemmas, proved here so that no Mathlib signature is load-bearing -/

/-- `map` fuses: this is `List.map_map`, re-proved to keep the file self-contained. -/
private theorem embMapMap {α β γ : Type} (g : β → γ) (f : α → β) (l : List α) :
    (l.map f).map g = l.map (fun a => g (f a)) := by
  induction l with
  | nil => rfl
  | cons a t ih =>
      show g (f a) :: ((t.map f).map g) = g (f a) :: t.map (fun a => g (f a))
      rw [ih]

/-- `map` preserves length: this is `List.length_map`, re-proved for the same reason. -/
private theorem embLenMap {α β : Type} (f : α → β) (l : List α) :
    (l.map f).length = l.length := by
  induction l with
  | nil => rfl
  | cons a t ih =>
      show (t.map f).length + 1 = t.length + 1
      rw [ih]

/-! ### The layer and circuit encodings of an embedded object -/

/-- The embedded layer's encoding keeps the ORIGINAL layer's length as its prefix. -/
private theorem embLayerEnc {m n : ℕ} (e : Fin m → Fin n) (he : Function.Injective e)
    (l : List (ShiShallow.Instr m)) :
    ShiBQP.encLayer (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l)
      = ShiBQP.encNat l.length
          ++ (l.map (fun g => ShiBQP.encInstr (ShiEmbed.embedInstr e he g))).flatten := by
  have hm : (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l).map ShiBQP.encInstr
      = l.map (fun g => ShiBQP.encInstr (ShiEmbed.embedInstr e he g)) :=
    embMapMap ShiBQP.encInstr (ShiEmbed.embedInstr e he) l
  show ShiBQP.encNat ((ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l).map ShiBQP.encInstr).length
      ++ ((ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l).map ShiBQP.encInstr).flatten = _
  rw [hm, embLenMap]

/-- The embedded circuit's encoding keeps the ORIGINAL circuit's layer count as its prefix. -/
private theorem embCircEnc {m n : ℕ} (e : Fin m → Fin n) (he : Function.Injective e)
    (c : ShiShallow.Layered m) :
    ShiBQP.encCirc (ShiEmbed.embedCirc e he c)
      = ShiBQP.encNat c.length
          ++ (c.map (fun l => ShiBQP.encLayer (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l))).flatten := by
  have hm : (ShiEmbed.embedCirc e he c).map ShiBQP.encLayer
      = c.map (fun l => ShiBQP.encLayer (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l)) :=
    embMapMap ShiBQP.encLayer (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he) c
  show ShiBQP.encNat ((ShiEmbed.embedCirc e he c).map ShiBQP.encLayer).length
      ++ ((ShiEmbed.embedCirc e he c).map ShiBQP.encLayer).flatten = _
  rw [hm, embLenMap]

/-- The per-layer INSTRUCTION COUNTS are unchanged, layer by layer. -/
private theorem embLenList {m n : ℕ} (e : Fin m → Fin n) (he : Function.Injective e)
    (c : ShiShallow.Layered m) :
    (ShiEmbed.embedCirc e he c).map List.length = c.map List.length := by
  have hm : (ShiEmbed.embedCirc e he c).map List.length
      = c.map (fun l => (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l).length) :=
    embMapMap List.length (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he) c
  have hf : (fun l : List (ShiShallow.Instr m) => (ShiEmbed.embedLayer e he l).length)
      = (fun l : List (ShiShallow.Instr m) => l.length) :=
    funext (fun l => embLenMap (ShiEmbed.embedInstr e he) l)
  rw [hm, hf]

/-! ### The concrete embedding `Fin 2 → Fin 5`, `i ↦ i + 2` -/

private def embE : Fin 2 → Fin 5 := fun i => ⟨i.val + 2, by omega⟩

private theorem embEinj : Function.Injective embE := by
  intro a b h
  obtain ⟨av, ha⟩ := a
  obtain ⟨bv, hb⟩ := b
  have h3 : av + 2 = bv + 2 := congrArg Fin.val h
  have h4 : av = bv := by omega
  subst h4
  rfl

/-! ### The public statement -/

theorem _root_.QMAReferenceValidation.candidate63 :
    (∀ (m n : ℕ) (e : Fin m → Fin n) (he : Function.Injective e),
